+
+Esc stopped in all three repetitions at 14 of the 16 budgets (not at 2.5 and 3\,GB). The calls saved grow with the budget, from 6\% at 250\,MB to 28\% at 15\,GB, because the plain search keeps spending calls while the improvement has stopped growing. The relative cost is the same as without Esc to within 0.2 points (at 6.5\,GB 68.1 against 67.9\%; at 4.25\,GB and 5.75\,GB it is lower, 70.6 against 71.5\% and 69.8 against 70.5\%). The paper reports about 60\% savings for two-phase greedy on TPC-H with SQL Server; we get at most 28\%. We have not measured how much of the stopping is due to the storage-aware bound on TPC-H; \texttt{configs/tpch\_sf10\_esc\_ablation.json} runs the same sweep with the count-only bound for that check.
+
+Esc saves what-if calls, not wall-clock time, here: a HypoPG what-if call costs only a millisecond or two, and the checker's simulated greedy at $K=100$ costs about as much. Esc's runtime is higher than the plain search at 9 of 16 budgets (for instance 8.4 against 4.1\,s at 8\,GB) and lower at 5 (3.5 against 4.9\,s at 15\,GB). Combined with Wii, Esc stopped only at budgets up to 2\,GB (and in two of three repetitions at 3.5\,GB), and saved at most two calls (407 against 405 at 3.5\,GB); at larger budgets it never stopped.
